        "kind": "math",
        "text": "Prior/update/interval: persistence prior = May annual CPI of 4.0%, using the December-May historical sample 3.8, 3.8, 3.7, 4.6, 4.2, 4.0. Successive changes are 0.0, -0.1, +0.9, -0.4, and -0.2 percentage point; their sample sigma = 0.50. Because only five changes were available in the fetched pre-resolution history, 1.28*sigma = 1.28*0.50 = 0.64 point is a short-sample normal-approximation half-width, not an empirical 80% quantile. Adjustments are -0.1 for recent easing, +0.1 for persistent Housing/electricity pressure, and 0.0 net for other one-offs, leaving 4.0%. The ladder implies bounds of 3.3% and 4.8%, total width 1.5 points versus the sigma-based 1.28 points; the 1.17x widening is judgmental allowance for rebate and base-effect volatility."
